 # Feed-forward and Generalizable Prediction
 
-Also called: Feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting, Generalizable 3DGS, Instant 3DGS
+Also called: Feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting, Generalizable 3DGS, One-shot 3D Reconstruction
 
 ## Overview
-Feed-forward and Generalizable Prediction refers to a family of methods that predict a complete 3D Gaussian Splatting scene representation directly from one or more input images in a single neural network forward pass, eliminating the need for per-scene gradient-based optimization . Unlike traditional 3D Gaussian Splatting which requires iterative optimization to fit a specific scene, these methods learn priors from large-scale datasets to generalize to unseen scenes . They distinguish themselves from sibling methods by their specific architectural choices for handling multi-view consistency, pose estimation, and primitive types (2D vs 3D), as detailed in the subtopics.
+Feed-forward and generalizable prediction methods map input images directly to a 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation without per-scene optimization [S1]. This family solves the latency bottleneck of traditional 3DGS, which requires iterative gradient descent for each new scene, by leveraging learned priors from large-scale datasets to predict Gaussian parameters in a single forward pass [Parent]. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on specific input constraints (e.g., calibrated poses or stereo pairs), this family encompasses diverse architectures ranging from uncalibrated stereo inputs to sparse multi-view setups, distinguished by their specific strategies for feature aggregation, spatial grouping, and primitive prediction [Parent].
 
