 Occam's LGS is a training-free method for lifting 2D language features to 3D Gaussian representations by applying Occam’s razor to the forward rendering process of 3D Gaussian Splatting. It solves the problem of expensive iterative optimization in feature space by computing weighted multi-view feature aggregation directly from the alpha blending weights of the standard color rendering pass. Unlike sibling methods that rely on scene-specific autoencoders, quantization, or costly semantic feature rendering, Occam's LGS supports arbitrary-dimensional uncompressed features without compromising training speed, achieving a two-order-of-magnitude speed-up while maintaining state-of-the-art performance for open-set vision-language tasks.
 
-### [Generalizable Pixel-Wise 3D Gaussian Splatting](17059-generalizable-pixel-wise-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
-GPS-Gaussian is a feed-forward method for real-time novel view synthesis of humans from sparse camera views. It directly regresses Gaussian parameters in a feed-forward manner from source views, eliminating the need for per-subject optimization or fine-tuning. Unlike original 3D-GS which requires per-subject optimization, GPS-Gaussian is instant and generalizable. It builds on [Feed-forward and Generalizable Prediction](15832-feed-forward-and-generalizable-prediction.md) by defining 2D Gaussian parameter maps on source view image planes, where each foreground pixel corresponds to a specific Gaussian point, allowing the use of efficient 2D convolution networks.
